<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pietro Badoglio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pietro Badoglio was an Italian marshal and politician. He commanded the final phase of the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1936, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a>, and was chief of the general staff until 1940. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s fall in July 1943 he became prime minister, concluded the armistice with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and fled with the king to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a>, resigning in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pietro-badoglio.jpg" alt="Pietro Badoglio" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_Badoglio_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pietro Badoglio was born on 28 September 1871, the son of a modest landowner. He entered the Royal Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1888 and became a second lieutenant in 1890. He served in the artillery, took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italo-ethiopian-war">First Italo-Ethiopian War</a> in 1896 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> of 1911 and 1912, and was decorated for the action at Ain Zara. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he rose from lieutenant colonel to major general after the capture of Monte Sabotino in May 1916. He was named vice-chief of staff in late 1917, although he was one of those chiefly responsible for the disaster at Caporetto on 24 October 1917. A commission of inquiry rejected most of the criticisms of him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Badoglio became a senator after the war and at first opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>, but he returned to a senior army role and was chief of staff from 4 May 1925. He was made Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on 25 June 1926. As the first sole governor of Tripolitania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, from 1929 to 1933, he took a leading part, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodolfo-graziani">Rodolfo Graziani</a>, in the campaign against Libyan resistance. By 1931 well over half of the population of Cyrenaica was confined to 15 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>, where many died, while his air force used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a> against the rebels.</p><p>On 3 October 1935 Badoglio replaced Emilio de Bono as commander of the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. He asked for and received permission to use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a>, and he used mustard gas against the Ethiopian armies. After the Battle of Maychew on 31 March 1936 he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> and entered it on 5 May 1936, and was made first viceroy and Duke of Addis Ababa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Badoglio resigned as chief of staff after the Italian army's poor performance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in December 1940. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-of-fascism">Fascist Grand Council</a> voted against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> on 24 July 1943 and the king dismissed him, Badoglio became prime minister. His representative signed the armistice with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> at Cassibile on 3 September 1943, and after the Allies announced it on 8 September, Badoglio and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a>. On 13 October his government declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and he resigned in June 1944, when Ivanoe Bonomi took his place. He was never tried for Italian war crimes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He died at Grazzano Badoglio on 1 November 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pietro Badoglio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Second Italo-Ethiopian War of 1935–1936, led by Emilio De Bono and then <b>Pietro Badoglio</b>, ended in the conquest of Ethiopia through numerical superiority, mechanised forces, aerial bombardment and the use of chemical weapons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Allied landing in Sicily in July 1943, the Grand Council of Fascism voted against Mussolini on the night of 24–25 July, and Victor Emmanuel III dismissed and arrested him, appointing Marshal <b>Pietro Badoglio</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Five weeks after its armistice with the Allies, <b>Pietro Badoglio</b>'s Italian government declared war on its former Axis partner.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-13">13 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Grazzano Badoglio, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Grazzano Badoglio, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.04,8.31&amp;year=1926" aria-label="Grazzano Badoglio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4748 573 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pietro Badoglio"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5198 843h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5198 843h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.04,8.31&amp;year=1926">What was here in 1926? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
